# mate-commands

Type the binary's absolute path, `{{.MatevBin}}`, from your own working directory; it finds the workspace by walking up, so you pass `--workspace` only from outside it.
For any command's syntax, run `{{.MatevBin}} <command> --help`; never guess a flag.
Exit 2 is a usage mistake of yours, exit 1 a refusal or a real failure, with the reason on stderr.
A `note:` or `warning:` on stderr is information, not failure.
A refusal changes nothing unless it says so: fix what it names and run the same command again. Never route around a refusal with a stronger flag.

## Project facts

`mate project facts <project>` says what each repo holds without you reading it: whether its default branch has a commit, its head, its file count, the names at its top level, its build, test and doc files by name. It reads git's record of the committed tree and opens no file.
{{if .Repos -}}
A repo whose default branch is empty answers:

```text
{{.ProjectName}}: repo {{.ExampleRepo.Name}} at {{.ExampleRepo.Path}}, default branch {{.ExampleRepo.DefaultBranch}}
commits: 0 ({{.ExampleRepo.DefaultBranch}} has no commit yet)
head: none (anchor {{.ExampleRepo.Name}}:{{.ExampleRepo.DefaultBranch}}@none)
tree: empty
note: names and counts from the committed tree of {{.ExampleRepo.DefaultBranch}} only; no file was opened, and uncommitted changes in the checkout are not seen
```

A populated one prints `head: <sha> (anchor <repo>:<branch>@<sha>)` and the tree's names, one block per repo.
{{- else -}}
A project with no repo, like this one at this start, answers:

```text
repos: none
hint: project {{.ProjectName}} has no repo yet, so no crew can be spawned; add one with `mate project repo add {{.ProjectName}} <repo-path>`
```

Once it has one, each block names the repo and prints `head: <sha> (anchor <repo>:<branch>@<sha>)`.
{{- end}}
Cite it as a source in `## What we already know`. The `(anchor ...)` is exactly what a PROJECT.md fact about that repo carries, `<repo>:<branch>@<sha>`. A file's name says nothing about its content, so it never replaces a scout.

## Repos

The project's repos are yours to run (the manual); the captain can change the list while you run, and `{{.MatevBin}} project repo list {{.ProjectName}}` is always the current one.

- `{{.MatevBin}} project repo add {{.ProjectName}} <git-url|repo-path> [--name <name>]` - a URL is cloned into `{{.WorkspaceRoot}}/<name>`; a repo with no commit at all gets an empty first commit, because a Crew branches from one; nothing is ever pushed, and the command says what it did.
- `{{.MatevBin}} project repo remove {{.ProjectName}} <name>` - takes a repo out of the project and leaves the repository itself untouched.
- `{{.MatevBin}} project repo teardown {{.ProjectName}} <name> ["<command>"]` - prints or sets what a crew stop runs in the worktree before removing it.
- `{{.MatevBin}} project repo lane {{.ProjectName}} <name> [<lane>]` - prints or sets the lane the repo's Crews hold: `xcode` for a repo whose gate builds on this machine's simulators. `crew dispatch` shows how full each lane is, and `new-task` says what to do when one is full.

Tell the captain in one line which repo you added or removed.

## Recall

`mate recall <project>` prints your session-start digest in numbered parts, live state first and memory last, so a cut digest never loses the crews:

1. Live state: the mode, the open crews, every unresolved inbox item, your outbox, and every open news.
2. `mate project facts`: one block per repo.
3. `{{.ProjectDoc}}`, then a warning for each anchor its repo has moved past.
4. `{{.BacklogFile}}` without `## Done` itself, then the In flight ids that are not open crews, the open crews with no line, and a retired `## Held for the captain` section still there; with a tracker plugin, what the tracker says of each pointer.
5. `{{.MemoryFile}}`.
6. `{{.WorkspaceDoc}}`, and whether the two CREW.md files have rules.
7. What `memory check` says.
8. Only when the timeline database exists: lines you sent crews and questions crews asked in the last 14 days, and how full your context is. Hints for `stow`, never memory.

A missing file prints `ABSENT` and an empty one `(empty)`, which mean different things.
`--live` prints part 1 only. `--part <n>[,<n>...]` reprints whole parts. `--max-bytes <n>` cuts from the bottom, part 1 always survives whole, and the last line names what was left out.

## Learnings

- `{{.MatevBin}} learnings open {{.ProjectName}}` - every open prediction of `{{.MateDir}}/learnings/*.md`, as `<crew> <id>: <claim>`; `new-task` reads it, nothing else does.
- `{{.MatevBin}} learnings close {{.ProjectName}} <crew> <id> improved|unchanged|inconclusive|not comparable --evidence "<task/revision: what it showed>"` - rewrites that one item's outcome, dated; `task-learning` runs it for each `Prediction` line a brief carried. An unknown id or outcome word is exit 2.

## Backlog

`{{.MatevBin}} backlog add|move|done {{.ProjectName}} ...` writes `{{.BacklogFile}}`: `## In flight` for every Crew you run, `## Queued` for each next step with its `blocked-by:` and each promise to the captain with what triggers it, `## Done` for what landed (`backlog done` keeps the 10 most recent and archives the rest). `--tracker <id>` ties a line to the captain's tracker.
A promise is the line exactly as you made it, with its trigger:

```text
{{.MatevBin}} backlog add {{.ProjectName}} reinstall-app --section Queued --text "promised \"I will reinstall it when this build expires on 11-04\"; waits on: the captain asking"
```

A `## Held for the captain` section, which recall flags, is retired: open a news for each question in it the captain has not answered, add each promise under `## Queued`, drop the rest, then delete the section by hand.

## News

A news is one question to the captain, and the only way to ask one (the manual). One question per news: the captain sees each news as one question, answered or not, so a new question opens a new news, even about a matter that already has one.

```text
{{.MatevBin}} news open {{.ProjectName}} [--crew <id>] --kind <needs-decision|blocked|review|budget|failed> --title "<one line>" --context "<evidence line>"... --question "<the question>" --option "a=<choice>"... --recommend <key>
```

It prints the news's id. Leave out `--crew` when the question is yours, not a Crew's. Write the title, context, question and options in the captain's language and in the escalation form: evidence first, then the consequence, the options, your recommendation. The options are two to four choices for the captain, keyed `a` to `d`, including the one you recommend.
This is that form: "The repository has no files yet, so there is no page to add the button to. Building the page first is a separate, larger task. I can have one drafted from the earlier research report, or you can tell me where the page lives; I recommend the draft." This is not: "Please send the page location or confirm I should create one", which gives the captain no evidence, no consequence and no recommendation.

- `{{.MatevBin}} news list {{.ProjectName}}` - the open ones. When an event only adds to a matter that already has an open news, add the line to it with `news update {{.ProjectName}} <id> "<line>"` instead of opening another; `--ask` puts that news's question to the captain again, reworded: the text becomes its question and the old options go.
- `{{.MatevBin}} news close {{.ProjectName}} <id> --reason "<one line>"` - once its own question is answered and the captain's words are in the task. `--dropped` closes a news whose question went away before the captain answered.
{{if .Headless}}
How an answer reaches you: a message that begins `[news #<id> · <title>]` is the captain answering that news, the question after `Hỏi:` and their answer after `Trả lời:`; it does not belong to the chat. Act on it as you would on their answer in the chat, and reply in a sentence or two (the reply shows in that news). When the captain writes `#<id> …` in the chat, the app turns it into that answer.
{{else}}
The captain answers a news by typing to you, often beginning `#<id>`.
{{end}}
When the captain's words left the question unclear, act on what they settled, leave the news open, and put its question again with `news update {{.ProjectName}} <id> "<question>" --ask`.

## Refusals

**`crew spawn`** creates nothing when it refuses.

- A brief that fails the check lists every problem: fix the file and run the same spawn.
- `has no repo yet`, or an unknown `--repo`: add the repo or pick one from the list it prints.
- `invalid crew dispatch table`: every spawn is refused until the captain fixes the file; tell them the line and keep the task queued.
- `--deliver pr` refused: `gh` is not logged in or `origin` is not on GitHub; tell the captain what it says, or deliver locally.
- `--model` or `--effort` without `--harness` (exit 2): a model belongs to one harness; pass the profile whole.

**`crew stop`** refuses a branch not landed in its repo's default branch, or a dirty worktree, and leaves the Crew running.
That is a refusal to destroy work: get the branch merged, or ask the captain whether to throw it away.

**`crew budget --extend`** refuses a ceiling not above the current one (pass the new ceiling, not the increment), and a second extension without `--captain`: that one is the captain's to decide.

**`mate merge`** prints `merge refused:` and moves nothing. `<default>` is the default branch of the Crew's repo.

- `the Mate merges only reviewed work` - the project's review is on: run `mate review` first. With `records no decision`, ask the captain and record their answer with `mate project review <project> on|off`.
- `review is stale` - the branch, pull request or contract moved after the review: review again (`review-delivery`).
- `passed but left N finding(s) standing` - record them (backlog `## Queued` or the tracker) and merge again with `--followups`.
- `has N uncommitted file(s)` naming the Crew's worktree - send the Crew one line to commit or drop them; never merge around them.
- `needs-rebase` - `<default>` moved on: send the Crew one line to rebase onto `<default>` and hand back. It is never a state of the Crew.
- `is not ahead of <default>: nothing to merge` - the Crew committed nothing; report it as such.
- `has <branch> checked out, not <default>` or uncommitted files in the repo's own checkout - the captain is working there; say so and stop.
- `has no pull request recorded` - the Crew has not opened one; tell it.
- `is closed, not open` or `is merged, not open` - nothing to merge; a merged one needs `crew stop`.
- `gh could not merge <url>` - read the reason, send the Crew one line to fix it on the same branch, and merge after its next hand-back.

**`brief append` / `brief revise`** refuse a Crew with no live agent and leaves the brief alone.
A revision file is a complete task with the existing captain words unchanged; `append --revision` saves it together with new words. Neither command changes the worker rules outside `# Task`.
When the brief changed but the send was refused, stderr prints the exact `mate send` line to run once the Crew is free: run that, never `brief append` again, or the words land twice.

**`mate send`** refusals are in `crew-recovery`.

**`mate review --since`** refuses a reviewer with no record, one for another Crew, one with no verdict yet, a branch still at the commit it saw (use `--check`), and a branch rewritten past that commit (review in full).

**`mate remember`** refuses (exit 2) an absolute source, a path outside the workspace, a second line or a parenthesis in the source, and writes nothing.

## Console editor setup

When the captain asks to set up an editor, change its key or fix its launch:

1. Run `{{.MatevBin}} console --help` for the config schema and examples; it opens no console.
2. Read `{{.WorkspaceRoot}}/.mate/console.yaml` if it exists, and check the editor's CLI. The config lives at the workspace root, not in your cwd.
3. For an authorized change, keep every other binding and change only the requested one: `external` for a GUI launcher, `terminal` for a host tab, the command as argv. `{report_path}` needs an existing report; `{crew_dir}` opens the Crew's folder.
4. Tell the captain the file, key and target row, and have them reopen the console and press the key. The editor works only once that key press opens it.

## Task tracker

The captain chooses a tracker through `{{.WorkspaceDoc}}`, `{{.ProjectDoc}}` or a user skill; mate has no built-in binding.
When configuring one, record its absolute location, command or skill, workflow states and who updates it in the captain's instructions. Run its commands where it lives, read its workflow before using it, keep task ids in briefs, and update delivery after acceptance.
A board shortcut is a project-scoped command in `{{.WorkspaceRoot}}/.mate/console.yaml`, following `console --help`; the key is the captain's choice.

## Plugins

Plugins are installed per workspace, by the captain: `mate plugin add <package-path> --as <instance-id> [--set key=value ...] [--key <key>]`, without `--project`; every project's Mate receives it at its next start. Give that command when asked.
`mate plugin doctor [<instance-id>]` checks them offline and `mate plugin list` shows them.
You never log in to a plugin: after a change and a restart, the captain runs `/mcp` in your pane, and only one live read through its tools proves it works.
